<!--
  Stage 3 example custom detection.

  This is the file you edit. It maps to Wazuh's own supported extension
  point (/var/ossec/etc/rules/local_rules.xml, already included by the
  shipped default ossec.conf) - so adding detections here never touches
  Wazuh's own core ruleset. Add your own rules below the example, using
  IDs 100000+ (that range is reserved for local/custom rules, so you can
  never collide with the shipped ruleset).

  Load it with ./reload-rules.sh, which validates the ruleset before it
  restarts the manager - a syntax error rolls back instead of taking the
  manager down. Test any rule with ./test-rules.sh (wazuh-logtest) before
  trusting it. See README.md.
-->
<group name="local,syslog,sshd,">

  <!--
    Detection: successful SSH login to the 'student' account.

    Why this one, and not "detect SSH bruteforce"? Wazuh's default ruleset
    already does that (rule 5763, "brute force trying to get access",
    built on the per-failure rule 5760) - writing our own version of
    something that already exists teaches nothing. This rule detects
    something the default ruleset has no way to know: that THIS SPECIFIC
    account, in THIS SPECIFIC lab, is a deliberately weak one nobody
    should be routinely logging into. That environment-specific context -
    which accounts/hosts/behaviours are unusual for YOUR estate - is
    exactly what custom detection engineering is for.

    ATT&CK: T1078 (Valid Accounts) - an adversary using a legitimate
    account's credentials to gain access.

    Parent rule: 5715 "sshd: authentication success" (shipped ruleset,
    0095-sshd_rules.xml). We add a field match on top of a rule Wazuh
    already provides - we don't reimplement SSH log parsing. Rule 5715's
    own level is 3 (informational); we raise this specific case to 10.
  -->
  <rule id="100010" level="10">
    <if_sid>5715</if_sid>
    <user>^student$</user>
    <description>Successful SSH login to the monitored 'student' account</description>
    <mitre>
      <id>T1078</id>
    </mitre>
    <group>local_detection,valid_accounts,</group>
  </rule>

  <!-- Add your own detections below this line. -->

</group>
